WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
The TritonAI Harness shows files on the left, the update request in the center, and the private preview on the right.

00:07.000 --> 00:15.000
The contributor requests one change: revise Where to start, keep both links, run checks, refresh the preview, and stop before GitHub or publishing.

00:15.000 --> 00:25.000
The website maintenance skill, called ucsd-website-maintainer, finds the file that controls the page, reads its update rules, and checks the existing links before editing.

00:25.000 --> 00:36.000
With approved voice context, voice-match follows the intended voice and humanizer removes generic AI phrasing. The branding skill keeps layout work aligned with UC San Diego.

00:36.000 --> 00:48.000
The accessibility skill, called ucsd-accessibility-compliance, checks headings and structure, keyboard use, phone layouts, and common automated accessibility problems while other required checks run.

00:48.000 --> 00:59.000
The browser skill refreshes the private preview, compares computer and phone layouts, confirms the updated text, and checks that both existing links still work.

00:59.000 --> 01:12.166
The local edit is ready for human review. Nothing has been committed, pushed, submitted as a pull request, merged, or published; the contributor decides whether to continue.

01:12.166 --> 01:20.166
After approval, the auto-review skill, tritonai-autoreview, checks for missed issues. The GitHub skill, github:yeet, prepares the pull request and monitors checks without merging.

01:20.166 --> 01:28.166
Pull request 120 shows the change, passed checks, affected page, and recovery steps. An authorized person decides whether to merge it.

01:28.166 --> 01:35.166
The Files changed tab shows every edited file and line. Reviewers confirm that it contains only the approved website change.

01:35.166 --> 01:43.166
The merge starts the website publishing workflow, called cascade-upload.yml. It runs three steps in order: build and plan, upload to Cascade, and publish.

01:43.166 --> 01:51.166
The first step prepares the software, builds and checks the site, confirms production settings, and tests the Cascade transfer without changing the live site.

01:51.166 --> 01:58.166
The second step sends approved files to Cascade. Delivery means Cascade received them; the public site may not have changed yet.

01:58.166 --> 02:05.166
The final step asks Cascade to publish. A successful workflow still does not prove that the public page changed.

02:05.166 --> 02:15.166
Finally, the Harness or contributor opens the production URL and compares it with the approved preview. Only after the public page matches is the update reported as live verified.
